"# What is an RDD in Apache Spark?\n\nRDD (Resilient Distributed Dataset) is Spark's fundamental, low-level data abstraction. It represents an immutable, fault-tolerant collection of data distributed across multiple partitions that can be processed in parallel across a Spark cluster.\n\nRDDs provide the foundation for distributed data processing in Spark and support operations such as `map()`, `filter()`, `flatMap()`, and `reduce()`.\n\n## 1. Understanding RDD — Resilient Distributed Dataset\n\n| Term        | Meaning                                                                                             |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Resilient   | Fault-tolerant. Spark can recompute lost partitions using lineage information if an Executor fails. |\n| Distributed | Data is divided into partitions that can be processed across multiple Executors.                    |\n| Dataset     | A collection of data elements such as numbers, strings, tuples, or objects.                         |\n\n## 2. Key Characteristics of RDD\n\n### 1. Immutable\n\nOnce an RDD is created, its contents cannot be modified in place through RDD transformations.\n\nEvery transformation creates a new RDD.\n\n```\nrdd1 = sc.parallelize([1, 2, 3, 4])\nrdd2 = rdd1.map(lambda x: x * 2)\n```\n\n- `rdd1` represents `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.\n- `rdd2` represents `[2, 4, 6, 8]`.\n- `rdd1` remains unchanged.\n\n### 2. Distributed and partitioned\n\nAn RDD is divided into logical partitions, allowing Spark to process different portions of the dataset in parallel.\n\n[spark-reference:rdd-partitions]\n\nEach partition can be processed by a separate task. Tasks execute in parallel when sufficient Executor resources are available.\n\n### 3. Fault-tolerant\n\nRDDs maintain lineage information, which describes the transformations used to derive them.\n\nIf an Executor fails and an RDD partition is lost, Spark can reconstruct that partition by re-executing the required transformations.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nRDD1 (Original Data)\n        |\n      filter()\n        |\nRDD2 (Filtered Data)\n        |\n       map()\n        |\nRDD3 (Transformed Data)\n```\n\nIf a partition of `RDD3` is lost, Spark can use its lineage to recompute the lost partition from the necessary parent data.\n\nImportant: Fault tolerance does not require the entire RDD to be duplicated across machines.\n\n### 4. Lazy evaluation\n\nRDD transformations are evaluated lazily.\n\nSpark records the transformations but does not execute them until an action is triggered.\n\n```\nrdd = sc.parallelize([1, 2, 3, 4])\nrdd2 = rdd.map(lambda x: x * 2)  # Transformation\nresult = rdd2.collect()          # Action\n```\n\nThe `map()` operation defines a transformation, while `collect()` triggers its execution and returns the results to the Driver.\n\n### 5. In-memory caching\n\nRDDs support `cache()` and `persist()` to reuse computed partitions.\n\n```\nrdd = sc.parallelize(range(1000000))\nrdd2 = rdd.map(lambda x: x * 2)\nrdd2.cache()\n\nrdd2.count()   # Computes and caches the RDD\nrdd2.take(10)  # Can reuse cached partitions\n```\n\nCaching is useful when the same dataset is accessed repeatedly. By default, RDDs are not automatically cached.\n\n## 3. How do we create an RDD?\n\nRDDs can be created using existing collections, external data sources, or transformations on other RDDs.\n\n### Method 1: Using `parallelize()`\n\n```\nrdd = sc.parallelize([10, 20, 30, 40], 2)\n```\n\nCreates an RDD with two partitions.\n\n### Method 2: Reading external data\n\n```\nrdd = sc.textFile(\"s3://bucket/data.txt\")\n```\n\nCreates an RDD where each element represents a line of text.\n\n### Method 3: Transforming an existing RDD\n\n```\nnew_rdd = rdd.filter(lambda x: x > 20)\n```\n\nCreates a new RDD from the original RDD.\n\n## 4. RDD Operations\n\nRDDs support two main categories of operations.\n\n| Transformations (Lazy) | Actions (Trigger execution) |\n| ---------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| `map()`                | `collect()`                 |\n| `flatMap()`            | `count()`                   |\n| `filter()`             | `first()`                   |\n| `distinct()`           | `take()`                    |\n| `reduceByKey()`        | `reduce()`                  |\n| `union()`              | `saveAsTextFile()`          |\n\nTransformations create new RDDs, while actions trigger computations to return results or write output.\n\n## 5. Is RDD still used in modern Spark?\n\nYes, but DataFrames and Spark SQL are generally preferred for structured data processing because they support automatic query optimization through Catalyst and efficient physical execution.\n\nRDDs are useful when developers need lower-level control over distributed data processing or must work with custom data structures and transformations.\n\n## Interview-ready answer (60 seconds)\n\n> RDD stands for Resilient Distributed Dataset. It is the fundamental, low-level data abstraction in Apache Spark and represents an immutable, partitioned, fault-tolerant collection of data that can be processed in parallel across multiple Executors.\n>\n> An RDD is resilient because Spark maintains lineage information, which allows lost partitions to be recomputed in case of failures. It is distributed because its data is divided into partitions that can be processed across different machines.\n>\n> RDDs are immutable, meaning transformations create new RDDs rather than modifying existing ones. They also support lazy evaluation, where transformations are executed only when an action is triggered.\n>\n> RDDs provide operations such as map, filter, flatMap, and reduce, and they support caching for efficient reuse of data.\n>\n> Although RDDs form the foundation of Spark's distributed execution model, DataFrames and Spark SQL are generally preferred for modern structured data processing because they provide additional query optimization capabilities.\n\nKey takeaway: RDD = Immutable + Distributed + Partitioned + Lazy + Fault-tolerant, with optional caching for efficient data reuse."
